{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-h2-reviewer-fable-38","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:78bfad17cf5492340eb0","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"The selected result (two-sample Student t-test over 60 weekly inspection records, t(58) = -41.994, p < 0.001) is not supported as stated: the 60 rows comprise only 12 independent units (module_id), each entered 5 times, so the reported degrees of freedom (58 instead of at most 10 at the module level), t-statistic, and p-value characterization overstate the effective sample size and precision. A module-level reanalysis using the report's own per-module means still yields a -3.000 percentage-point difference with approximately t(10) ≈ -17.9 (p < 0.001), so the direction and approximate magnitude of the effect are robust; only the row-level inferential statistics are invalid.","root_cause":"The workflow's losses() function returns one power_loss_pct value per CSV row and build_report() passes the pooled 30-vs-30 row-level lists directly to scipy.stats.ttest_ind with dof = len(treated) + len(control) - 2 = 58. No per-module aggregation or dependence-aware model is applied, so the five repeated weekly readings from each of the 12 independent modules (independent-unit column module_id, per data-description.md) are each entered as separate independent observations into a procedure that assumes row independence.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares module_id as the independent-unit column and states that each module contributes five rows describing the same physical hardware over time, establishing within-module dependence across rows.","observed_value":"12 independent modules, 5 rows each; independent unit = module_id","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":23,"end_line":26,"quoted_text":"Because the same twelve modules were revisited week after week rather than\nreplaced, each module supplies five separate rows to the file, and readings\nthat share a module label describe the same piece of hardware at different\npoints in time."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":28,"end_line":29,"quoted_text":"One row is: one weekly soiling inspection of one photovoltaic module\nIndependent unit column: module_id"}]},{"description":"The losses() helper collects one value per CSV row with no per-module aggregation, so the coating-level lists contain all five repeated readings from each module.","observed_value":"30 row-level values per coating group (6 modules x 5 weeks)","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":38,"end_line":45,"quoted_text":"    picked = []\n    for row in records:\n        if coating is not None and row[\"coating\"] != coating:\n            continue\n        if module_id is not None and row[\"module_id\"] != module_id:\n            continue\n        picked.append(float(row[\"power_loss_pct\"]))\n    return picked"}]},{"description":"The row-level lists are passed directly into scipy.stats.ttest_ind, a procedure that treats each entry as an independent observation, and degrees of freedom are computed from the row counts (60 - 2 = 58).","observed_value":"stats.ttest_ind on 30-vs-30 rows; dof = 58","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":62,"end_line":65,"quoted_text":"    treated = losses(records, coating=TREATED)\n    control = losses(records, coating=CONTROL)\n\n    outcome = stats.ttest_ind(treated, control, equal_var=True)"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":68,"end_line":68,"quoted_text":"    dof = len(treated) + len(control) - 2"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly states that each of the 60 inspection records was entered as one independent observation and reports the resulting row-level statistics as the selected result.","observed_value":"t(58) = -41.994, p < 0.001 over 60 records","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":36,"end_line":38,"quoted_text":"Two-sample Student t-test (equal variances assumed) comparing nanocoat against\nbare power-loss values, with each of the 60 inspection records entered as one\nindependent observation."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":41,"end_line":41,"quoted_text":"- t(58) = -41.994, p < 0.001"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":43,"end_line":43,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Two-sample Student t-test over 60 weekly inspection records: nanocoat modules averaged 2.000% soiling power loss versus 5.000% for bare modules, a difference of -3.000 percentage points (t(58) = -41.994, p < 0.001)."}]},{"description":"The input data confirms repeated entry of the same independent unit: e.g., module PV-C01 appears in five separate rows, one per inspection week.","observed_value":"5 rows for module_id PV-C01","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":6,"quoted_text":"PV-C01,nanocoat,1,1.5\nPV-C01,nanocoat,2,1.6\nPV-C01,nanocoat,3,1.7\nPV-C01,nanocoat,4,1.6\nPV-C01,nanocoat,5,1.6"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report's own per-module mean table shows the effect is consistent across every module, and a correct module-level reanalysis (n = 6 vs 6 module means) still gives a -3.000 percentage-point difference with approximately t(10) ≈ -17.9, p < 0.001. The qualitative conclusion therefore does not depend on the pseudoreplicated procedure; only the reported test statistics do.","observed_value":"module-level means: nanocoat 1.6-2.3%, bare 4.5-5.4%; module-level t ≈ -17.9 on 10 df","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":14,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"| PV-C01 | nanocoat | 5 | 1.600 |\n| PV-C02 | nanocoat | 5 | 1.800 |\n| PV-C03 | nanocoat | 5 | 2.000 |\n| PV-C04 | nanocoat | 5 | 2.100 |\n| PV-C05 | nanocoat | 5 | 2.300 |\n| PV-C06 | nanocoat | 5 | 2.200 |\n| PV-U01 | bare | 5 | 4.500 |\n| PV-U02 | bare | 5 | 4.800 |\n| PV-U03 | bare | 5 | 5.100 |\n| PV-U04 | bare | 5 | 5.000 |\n| PV-U05 | bare | 5 | 5.400 |\n| PV-U06 | bare | 5 | 5.200 |"}]},{"description":"The report transparently discloses its assumption that each inspection record is an independent observation and accurately describes the design (12 modules, 5 weekly inspections), so the flaw is an incorrect analysis choice openly stated rather than a concealed manipulation; this does not, however, make the stated t(58) inference valid.","observed_value":"disclosed row-independence assumption","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":5,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"Field trial at one utility-scale array: 12 modules (6 treated with the nanocoat\nanti-soiling layer, 6 bare controls) were inspected once per week for 5\nconsecutive weeks, yielding 60 inspection records. The response variable is"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":36,"end_line":38,"quoted_text":"Two-sample Student t-test (equal variances assumed) comparing nanocoat against\nbare power-loss values, with each of the 60 inspection records entered as one\nindependent observation."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the issue three ways. (1) I checked whether the workflow aggregates to one value per module before testing: losses() appends one value per CSV row and build_report() passes the resulting 30-element lists directly to stats.ttest_ind, with dof = 60 - 2 = 58, confirming row-level entry with no aggregation, clustering, or mixed-effects handling anywhere in analysis.py. (2) I checked whether rows sharing a module_id could plausibly be independent units: data-description.md explicitly states the same twelve modules were revisited weekly and names module_id as the independent-unit column, ruling this out. (3) I tested whether the flaw is materially consequential by recomputing the test at the module level from the report's per-module means (nanocoat: 1.6, 1.8, 2.0, 2.1, 2.3, 2.2; bare: 4.5, 4.8, 5.1, 5.0, 5.4, 5.2): the difference remains -3.000 points with pooled-variance t ≈ -17.9 on 10 df. The direction survives, but the reported t(58) = -41.994 and its implied precision do not match any valid unit-level analysis, so the falsification attempts failed to overturn the finding that the stated statistical result rests on repeated entry of independent units into a row-independent procedure.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}